WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when I was six my family moved to the  city of Hyderabad and Vivek was my new  neighbor  he quickly became one of my closest  friends he and I would run along the  road we play soccer in the evenings and  race our bikes down the nearby hill at  that age I thought we had a lot in  common but our lives couldn't have been  more different I lived with my family in  a small brick apartment with running  water and electricity Vivek lived in the  field next door in a tiny Shack made out  of thin sheets and tarpaulin for water  Vivek and his sisters would walk a mile  every day to collect it from the tank at  night they would burn wood scraps to  keep themselves warm and cook while I  was in school learning about history  math and science  Vivek would spend his days collecting  wood and water for his family while I  had the benefit of clean air at home his  home was surrounded by thick grey smoke  from the burning wood I really wanted to  help with it but I didn't know how so I  asked my mother and to my surprise she  offered to pay for his school and even  found one that he could go to but his  family had to decline our offer their  lack of access to energy meant that  Vivek had to stay at home and help his  family I was 10 when I first learned  that there simply wasn't enough energy  to go around when I wasn't playing with  Vivek and other friends during the  school year I would often visit my uncle  Moe Hans rice farm just like over half  the farmers in the country  his family lived off a small plot of  land about the size of an average  McDonald's parking lot in India the  government offers free electricity to  all farmers but that promised  electricity is extremely unreliable  because of this sometimes he couldn't  pump enough water to his crops other  times if the pumps were left on the  crops would be flooded either way this  meant that he would end up having to  discard about half of his rice supply  each season and without enough rice to  sell  he couldn't send my cousin's to better  schools or afford health care again I  wanted to help but I didn't know how it  seemed that there simply wasn't enough  energy to go around at that age I  couldn't help we wake or my uncle but as  I grew older I became determined to help  others like them at college I studied  energy systems after college I worked  for a large energy company and I learned  a lot from these experiences I learned  that we wake is one of 2 billion  children who rely on dirty sources of  energy 2 billion children who breathe in  dirty air and face asthma and other  chronic lung diseases 2 billion children  who are unable to go to school because  they have to collect scraps of wood 2  billion who do not have the opportunity  to rise out of poverty I also learnt  that my uncle is among hundreds of  millions of farmers who struggle to make  ends meet because of an unreliable  energy supply the scale of this problem  is daunting but this isn't the first  time that we've enabled large-scale  change think about the mobile banking  revolution in Africa  for decades millions of Africans did not  have access to banking services they  couldn't hold money in an account they  couldn't transfer funds and they did not  have access to credit but a few  innovative companies realized that while  they didn't have access to banks they  did have cell phones and this started a  revolution millions of families now have  access to financial services even in  remote parts of the developing world  access that has dramatically changed  their lives and help them rise out of  poverty what we did for access to  financial services we need to do for  energy mobile banking started with the  simple idea that traditional banks need  not be the only solution for financial  access we need to find similar no Bank  solutions for energy and apply those  learnings first we must identify the  real need for financial services the  need was not for a traditional bank but  for an easy way to transfer funds and  have access to credit for energy we need  to identify those fundamental needs and  under and build innovative solutions  around them the company embrace founded  by Stanford Business School alum  understood this for parents with  premature babies the real need was not  for expensive energy intensive  incubators in city hospitals they simply  needed a way to keep their babies warm  so embrace came up with an at-home  low-cost infant warmer saving over a  hundred thousand lives in the process  when it comes to energy access we need  more companies like embrace which  understand those fundamental needs and  build innovative solutions around them  second  we need to use existing technology to  address these needs we have so much  amazing technology here that we simply  need to find a way to apply it to the  developing world take fly bird for  example this company co-opted  decades-old sensor technology to build a  device that regulates the amount of  water and fertilizer that's delivered to  a crop this device solves the exact need  that my uncle had and is built on  existing technology when it comes to  energy access  we need companies like fly bird which  build solutions on existing platforms  lastly we need to start solving small  local problems and not just large  systemic ones mobile banking started by  creating solutions for families not  countries if we had waited for mobile  mobile banking and just banking services  to expand we would have lost the  opportunity to serve a whole generation  of the population systemic change is  slow but small-scale change can start  today micro grids are a great example of  one such solution these grids are built  in remote areas and provide energy  access to small communities a whole  series of such no Bank solutions can  help Vivek and other families like their  homes and send their kids to school it  can help my uncle and other farmers earn  a living wage this change is possible  let's make it happen because the truth  is there is enough energy to go around  we simply need to find the right way to  get it there  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

